(window.LADDER_CARDS["8·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8·phys.1.1","q":"The old book on counting sand set out to name a number larger than the grains that would fill the universe. What was its method?","a":"estimate the size of a grain and of the universe, multiply, and name the result in tiers of powers","w":["count a handful and guess","prove that the grains are endless","weigh the Earth"],"why":"His answer was fewer than 10⁶³ grains. The universe is far larger than he thought, and the method is this page’s."},
 {"u":"8·phys.2.1","q":"A positive quantity is written u · 10ⁿ. What pins the form down to exactly one?","a":"n is an integer and 1 ≤ u < 10","w":["u is a whole number","n is positive","u is as small as possible"],"why":"One-scientific-form, by counting-passes-any-real and a least member. n is the order of magnitude, the rung of sizes; u says where on that rung."},
 {"u":"8·phys.3.1","q":"A hair is 7 · 10⁻⁵ m thick and a hydrogen atom 1.1 · 10⁻¹⁰ m across. How many atoms fit across the hair?","a":"about 6 · 10⁵: divide, and the exponents subtract","w":["about 6 · 10¹⁵","about 8 · 10⁻¹⁵","about 6 · 10⁻⁵"],"why":"Ratios are where the ladder of sizes pays: both lengths in metres, then one division."},
 {"u":"8·phys.4.1","q":"The last written digit of a measurement says “measured to here”. Why keep figures, not places, for a product?","a":"in a product the errors grow as parts of the whole, and the count of figures roughly measures that part","w":["products have no decimal places","places are only for whole numbers","figures are easier to count"],"why":"2.4 m/s for 3.15 s gives 7.56 on paper, but the bounds on the two factors leave two figures: the factor with the biggest part wins."},
 {"u":"8·phys.5.1","q":"Why does a chain estimate usually land within a factor of a few?","a":"each guess is off by a factor, the factors multiply, and on the exponent scale the errors add and partly cancel","w":["each guess is exact","the errors are all too high","long chains use more data"],"why":"Some guesses are too high and some too low. The claim is modest: a hundred or two, not ten and not ten thousand."},
 {"u":"8·phys.6.1","q":"10³ · 10² = ?","a":"10⁵: exponents add when you multiply","w":["10⁶: exponents multiply","10⁹","2 · 10⁵"],"why":"Exponents multiply only for a power of a power, (10³)² = 10⁶. And 10³ + 10³ is 2 · 10³."},
 {"u":"8·phys.7.1","q":"Handed a measured product, what should you do before you round it?","a":"bound it: multiply the low ends together, and the high ends","w":["add the significant figures of the factors","write every digit the multiplication gives","double it for safety"],"why":"The bounds say which figures are measured and which are noise."});
/* end phase F und_r08 */
/* phase F und_r09: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["9·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["9·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"9·phys.1.1","q":"Why is position a function of time, and why need it not be one-to-one?","a":"nothing is in two places at once, so each instant has one place; a waiting bus has one place at many instants","w":["each place has one instant; a body never comes back","a body can be in two places at once; so it is one-to-one","position is chosen by the mark; it never changes"],"why":"No instant appears in two pairs (t, x): 9a's definition. Two instants may share an output, so the function need not run backwards."},
 {"u":"9·phys.2.1","q":"What does steady-motion say, and why does the old book insist on “any”?","a":"the same v on every stretch exactly when x(t) = x₀ + vt; equal distances in whole hours would let in hurrying and dawdling within each","w":["x(t) = vt² on every stretch; “any” is for style","the same v on some stretch is enough; “any” means one","x(t) = x₀ + vt only from rest; “any” means any body"],"why":"“Any equal intervals” is a for-all over stretches. Checked only hour by hour, a motion can pass and still not be steady."},
 {"u":"9·phys.3.1","q":"What is uniform acceleration, and what does this page hold in trust?","a":"from rest, equal amounts of speed gained in equal intervals, v(t) = kt; the speed at an instant, until 13·phys","w":["equal distances in equal intervals; the value of k","v(t) = kt²; the metre","speed that never changes; the mark on the track"],"why":"It is the definition of uniform motion moved up one level. The page uses the speed at an instant only through one plain fact about averages."},
 {"u":"9·phys.4.1","q":"How does falling-distance prove x(t) = ½kt² without anything past rung 8?","a":"cut the time into n equal steps; the distance is squeezed between two counted sums, and only ½kt² sits between them for every n","w":["measure a fall and fit the curve","take the last speed kt for the whole time","take one step only, with n = 1, and average its two ends"],"why":"The premise bounds each step by its starting and ending speeds. Counting-passes-any-real leaves no second number between the sums for every n."},
 {"u":"9·phys.5.1","q":"What do odd-numbers-make-squares and halfway-speed say?","a":"the first n odd numbers add to n²; the average velocity from t₁ to t₂ is the speed at the time halfway between","w":["the first n odd numbers add to 2n; the average is the final speed","the squares add to an odd number; the average is half the starting speed","the first n odd numbers add to n²; the average velocity is the speed at t₂"],"why":"Gaps of 1, 3, 5, 7 give totals 1, 4, 9, 16. And (½k(t₂² − t₁²))/(t₂ − t₁) = ½k(t₁ + t₂), by difference-of-squares."},
 {"u":"9·phys.6.1","q":"Why did the old experimenter use a raised board, and why does “whatever the height” matter?","a":"it slows the fall enough to time; the height changes k but not the square, since ½k cancels from every ratio","w":["a board removes the air; the height changes the square","a ball cannot fall freely; the height changes nothing at all","it speeds the fall up; the height sets the ratios 1, 3, 5, 7"],"why":"A free fall of 4.9 m takes one second. On the board the distances were as the squares of the times at every tilt."},
 {"u":"9·phys.7.1","q":"How is time found from distance, and why does the negative root belong to no instant?","a":"t = √(2x/a), the kept squaring run backwards; the fall was kept to t ≥ 0, so no instant is negative","w":["t = 2x/a; there is no root to take","t = √(2x/a) or −√(2x/a); both are instants of the fall","t = √(x/(2a)); the negative root is the way up"],"why":"On t ≥ 0 squaring is one-to-one, so root-undoes-square applies. A fall of 20 m takes √(40/9.8), about 2.0 s."},
 {"u":"9·phys.8.1","q":"Why is “final speed kt, so distance kt · t” a move to watch out for?","a":"it uses the last speed for the whole trip; the body was slower all the way, and falling-distance gives half","w":["the final speed is kt²","the distance is kt · t²","it is sound: speed times time is distance"],"why":"The premise puts the distance between 0 and kt · t. The average velocity is the halfway speed ½kt, so x = ½kt²."},
 {"u":"9·phys.9.1","q":"Handed a table of positions at equal times, how do you tell the motion?","a":"equal gaps mean uniform; gaps as 1, 3, 5, 7 mean uniformly accelerated from rest","w":["equal gaps mean accelerated; growing gaps mean uniform","read the last position only","equal positions mean uniform; equal gaps mean at rest"],"why":"Check “any” with a finer table. With 1, 3, 5, 7 the first gap is ½k per square second."});
/* end phase F und_r09 */
