(window.LADDER_CARDS["10·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10·phys.1.1","q":"How is the race cut into stages, and what is each stage's time?","a":"each stage runs until the runner reaches where the tortoise was when it began; t_k = (D/v) · q^k with q = u/v","w":["each stage is one second; t_k = 1","each stage is a lap; t_k = D/u","each stage halves the gap; t_k = (D/v) · k"],"why":"The gap at each stage is the last one times q, so d_k = D q^k, and time is distance over speed."},
 {"u":"10·phys.2.1","q":"What is T_n, the time when stage n − 1 ends?","a":"the first n stage times added: (D/v) · (1 − qⁿ)/(1 − q), by ratio-sum","w":["(D/v) · qⁿ","n · D/v","(D/v) · (1 − q)/(1 − qⁿ)"],"why":"With q = 1/10 and a first stage of 20 s the totals run 20, 22, 22.2, 22.22, heading for 200/9."},
 {"u":"10·phys.3.1","q":"What does powers-to-0 say, and how does one-plus-a-power prove it?","a":"for 0 ≤ q < 1, qⁿ → 0; with 1/q = 1 + h, (1/q)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nh > nh, so qⁿ < (1/h) · (1/n)","w":["qⁿ → 1; since 1ⁿ = 1","qⁿ → 0 for every q; by a table","qⁿ → q; since the base is fixed"],"why":"1/n heads to 0, and the squeeze traps qⁿ between 0 and a constant times 1/n."},
 {"u":"10·phys.4.1","q":"What does stage-sum say, and what does it make of the stage times?","a":"c(1 + q + ⋯ + q^(n−1)) → c/(1 − q) for 0 ≤ q < 1; the stage times add toward D/(v − u), and every stage ends before it","w":["the total passes every bound; the runner never arrives","the total is c · n; the stages take equal times","c(1 + q + ⋯ + q^(n−1)) → c/(1 − q); some stage ends exactly at D/(v − u)"],"why":"Ratio-sum, then powers-to-0. T_n = (D/(v − u))(1 − qⁿ), always short of the limit."},
 {"u":"10·phys.5.1","q":"How is the moment found directly, and what did the old argument miss?","a":"solve vt = D + ut for t* = D/(v − u); endless stages need not take endless time: it counted the stages and never added their times","w":["count the stages; it missed the tortoise's speed","solve vt = ut; it missed the head start","solve vt = D + ut; it missed that the tortoise stops"],"why":"Two roads, one answer. The direct road needs no limit at all; after t* the runner is ahead."},
 {"u":"10·phys.6.1","q":"Why is shrinking not enough, and what does grouped-halves say?","a":"1, ½, ⅓, ¼, … shrink to 0, yet 1 + ½ + ⋯ + 1/2^k ≥ 1 + k/2, so the totals pass every bound","w":["shrinking times always add to a finite total; the total is 2","the times 1, ½, ⅓ do not shrink; the total is 1 + k","1 + ½ + ⋯ + 1/2^k ≤ 2 for every k"],"why":"Each group from 1/(2^k + 1) to 1/2^(k+1) has 2^k terms, each at least 1/2^(k+1), so it adds at least ½."},
 {"u":"10·phys.7.1","q":"What does the Hayward Field chase show about the limit and the race?","a":"the limit gives the moment exactly, 100 s; the race decides whether it comes in time, and a lap takes only about 62 s","w":["the miler catches the leader in the first stage","the limit is never the moment of the catch","the stages end after 100 s, so she wins on the line"],"why":"t* = 20/(6.5 − 6.3). With q ≈ 0.969 the stages shrink slowly, and she runs out of track before the catch."},
 {"u":"10·phys.8.1","q":"Why is “the times shrink to 0, so their total is finite” a move to watch out for?","a":"grouped-halves: 1, ½, ⅓, … shrink to 0 and their totals pass every bound","w":["no run of times shrinks to 0","every total is finite","it is sound whenever the first time is 1"],"why":"What stage-sum needs is a fixed factor q < 1 from each time to the next."},
 {"u":"10·phys.9.1","q":"Handed a run of shrinking times, what question tells you whether the total is finite?","a":"is each at most a fixed q < 1 times the last? If so, the totals stay below t₀/(1 − q)","w":["is the first time below 1?","do the times head to 0?","is each shorter than the last?"],"why":"If they shrink by less and less, as 1/k does, nothing is promised."});
/* end phase F und_r10 */
